+"""Debug-only allocator telemetry for E2, BOUNDARY-ONLY. Off unless
+VLLM_E2_MEMSTATS=<ctl poll seconds> and VLLM_E2_MEMSTATS_DIR=<dir>.
+
+WHY THIS IS NOT A SAMPLER ANY MORE. An earlier version read
+torch.cuda.memory_stats() and reset_peak_memory_stats() every VLLM_E2_MEMSTATS
+seconds. Both take the per-device caching-allocator mutex and the Python
+bindings do not release the GIL, so the old contract's claim that this thread
+"takes no lock shared with the model path" was FALSE. Three one-boot arms at a
+4.1e9 KV pin showed a monotone dose-response: sampling at 4 Hz wedged a stage-0
+worker during a 131K thinking-on row; at 1.1 Hz it survived that row and wedged during
+a 260K row; with the thread disabled the run completed every row. Causation
+is not established at n=1 per arm (a known limit of
+that capacity probe), but polling has no place
+inside a measured region, and it was never necessary: PyTorch maintains the
+allocator high-water marks itself, so reading them once per window is exact.
+
+WHAT THIS THREAD DOES NOW. Between window boundaries it makes NO CUDA calls and
+touches NO allocator state: its tick is a plain read of <dir>/window.ctl, which
+holds "<seq> <name>". The gate driver crosses a boundary only while the server
+is quiet (no request running). On a new seq the thread performs these
+boundary-only allocator reads:
+  1. memory_stats()            -> the closing window's exact high-water marks
+                                  and cumulative retry/OOM counters
+  2. reset_peak_memory_stats() -> immediately after the read, with no file I/O
+                                  in the gap
   3. mem_get_info()            -> device free/total AT THAT QUIESCENT INSTANT
-  4. host_memory_stats()       -> pinned-host allocator current values, outside the read-to-reset gap
-then writes the "close" record for the old window and the "ack" record for the new one.
+  4. host_memory_stats()       -> pinned-host allocator current values, outside
